# Crockpot API — /me (profile, password, account deletion)
#
# Own file, own login: REST Client's captured variables and cookie jar
# are scoped per .http file, not shared across files, so this can't
# chain off requests/auth.http's login — it does its own, right at the
# top, using the same confirmed password account auth.http sets up (run
# its ✅ register + ✅ confirm sections once first if you haven't on
# this dev DB). Fill in your real email/password below.
#
# Also the real end-to-end proof that AuthMiddleware (CROC-003) works
# against a live request — no prior ticket exercised it outside its own
# unit tests. See requests/README.md for setup/running instructions.


# ══════════════════════════════════════════
#  GET /me
# ══════════════════════════════════════════

### Login, to get a token for the checks below.
# @name login
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_REAL_EMAIL",
  "password": "correcthorsebattery"
}

###

@authToken = {{login.response.body.accessToken}}

### ✅ 200 — returns the authenticated user's profile
GET http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me
Authorization: Bearer {{authToken}}

###

### 🔒 401 — no token
GET http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me

###

### 🔒 401 — malformed token
GET http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me
Authorization: Bearer not-a-valid-token


# ══════════════════════════════════════════
#  PATCH /me — rename
# ══════════════════════════════════════════

### ✅ 200 — trims and saves the name; the response is the /me shape,
### with authProvider "password" for this account.
PATCH http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me
Authorization: Bearer {{authToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "   REST Client Renamed   "
}

###

### ✅ 200 — the byline is a live lookup: createdByName on a recipe this
### account created shows the name set above. Paste the id of any recipe
### this account created (recipes.http creates some) below.
GET http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/recipes/REPLACE_WITH_A_RECIPE_ID_THIS_ACCOUNT_CREATED
Authorization: Bearer {{authToken}}

###

### ❌ 400 invalid_name — whitespace only
PATCH http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me
Authorization: Bearer {{authToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "   "
}

###

### ❌ 400 invalid_name — 51 characters
PATCH http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me
Authorization: Bearer {{authToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"
}


# ══════════════════════════════════════════
#  POST /me/password
#
#  Changes the shared account's password and then changes it back, so
#  the file's Login still works on the next run. A Google account's 409
#  no_password needs a Google session, so it's covered by handler tests
#  only. Each successful change sends a real "password changed" email.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════

### 🔒 403 invalid_password — wrong current password (403, not 401, so
### the frontend doesn't treat it as an expired session)
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me/password
Authorization: Bearer {{authToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "currentPassword": "not-the-password",
  "newPassword": "correcthorsebattery-changed"
}

###

### ❌ 400 password_too_short
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me/password
Authorization: Bearer {{authToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "currentPassword": "correcthorsebattery",
  "newPassword": "short"
}

###

### ✅ 200 — new accessToken + refreshToken cookie. Before running this,
### copy the refreshToken cookie value out of the Login response's
### Set-Cookie header into PRE_CHANGE_REFRESH_TOKEN below.
# @name changePassword
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me/password
Authorization: Bearer {{authToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "currentPassword": "correcthorsebattery",
  "newPassword": "correcthorsebattery-changed"
}

###

@changedAuthToken = {{changePassword.response.body.accessToken}}

### 🔒 401 — the pre-change session was revoked by the change.
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/refresh
Cookie: refreshToken=PRE_CHANGE_REFRESH_TOKEN

###

### ✅ 200 — the session the change issued (held by the cookie jar) works.
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/refresh

###

### 🔒 401 invalid_credentials — the old password no longer logs in
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_REAL_EMAIL",
  "password": "correcthorsebattery"
}

###

### ✅ 200 — change it back, so Login at the top works next run.
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me/password
Authorization: Bearer {{changedAuthToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "currentPassword": "correcthorsebattery-changed",
  "newPassword": "correcthorsebattery"
}

###

### 🚦 429 rate_limit_exceeded — send this 11 times within a minute; the
### 11th returns 429 (the 10/min auth limit). Earlier ones return 403.
### Uses up this minute's budget, so run it last.
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me/password
Authorization: Bearer {{changedAuthToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "currentPassword": "not-the-password",
  "newPassword": "correcthorsebattery-unused"
}


# ══════════════════════════════════════════
#  DELETE /me
#
#  Runs on its own throwaway account, never the shared one above. Use
#  an address you can read (e.g. a +alias of your own) for
#  REPLACE_WITH_A_THROWAWAY_EMAIL, and copy the code from its
#  confirmation email into THROWAWAY_CODE_FROM_EMAIL. What happens to
#  the account's recipes (drafts deleted, approved ones kept with no
#  byline) is covered by repository tests, since creating one here
#  needs recipes.http's items and categories.
#
#  Shares the 10/min limit with POST /me/password: wait a minute after
#  that section's 429 check before starting here.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════

### ✅ 201 — register the throwaway account
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "REPLACE_WITH_A_THROWAWAY_EMAIL",
  "password": "throwaway-horse-battery",
  "name": "Throwaway"
}

###

### ✅ 200 — confirm it with the emailed code
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/confirm
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "REPLACE_WITH_A_THROWAWAY_EMAIL",
  "code": "THROWAWAY_CODE_FROM_EMAIL"
}

###

### ✅ 200 — log in as it. Before running the delete below, copy the
### refreshToken cookie value from this response's Set-Cookie header
### into THROWAWAY_REFRESH_TOKEN.
# @name throwawayLogin
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "REPLACE_WITH_A_THROWAWAY_EMAIL",
  "password": "throwaway-horse-battery"
}

###

@throwawayToken = {{throwawayLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 🔒 403 invalid_password — nothing is deleted
DELETE http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me
Authorization: Bearer {{throwawayToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "not-the-password"
}

###

### ✅ 204 — deletes the account; the response clears the refresh cookie
### (Max-Age 0 in the response headers). Also proves a DELETE with a JSON
### body reaches the handler.
DELETE http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me
Authorization: Bearer {{throwawayToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "throwaway-horse-battery"
}

###

### 🔒 401 — the access token is still unexpired, but its user is gone
GET http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me
Authorization: Bearer {{throwawayToken}}

###

### 🔒 401 — the session's refresh token died with the account
POST http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/auth/refresh
Cookie: refreshToken=THROWAWAY_REFRESH_TOKEN

###

### 🚦 429 rate_limit_exceeded — send this 11 times within a minute; the
### 11th returns 429 (the 10/min auth limit, shared with POST
### /me/password). Earlier ones return 401, since the account is gone.
DELETE http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me
Authorization: Bearer {{throwawayToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "password": "throwaway-horse-battery"
}


# ══════════════════════════════════════════
#  Cleanup
# ══════════════════════════════════════════

### Re-run the Login request above once more first, so @authToken is
### freshly issued (well inside its 15-minute expiry) going into the
### check below — otherwise a 401 there could just mean the token
### expired, not that the user is gone.

### Deletes the same shared test account requests/auth.http uses — only
### run this once you're done testing against it for this session; run
### auth.http's own ✅ register + ✅ confirm sections again afterward
### before its account-dependent sections will work. Connect via
### psql/a DB client:
#
#   DELETE FROM users WHERE email = 'REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_REAL_EMAIL';
#

### 🔒 401 — GET /me with the now-stale @authToken from the freshly
### re-issued login above, run immediately after the DELETE. Proves the
### valid-token-but-deleted-user path (CROC-009's RefreshToken/Me fix)
### for real, not just against a mock.
GET http://localhost:{{$dotenv PORT}}/me
Authorization: Bearer {{authToken}}
